Z||000000||Formula 18||09-27-2000||12:24 AM||||Does the 88 Fieros share rotors with anyother car?? Something is way wrong with my rear rotors and it has causes the caliper to rub a DEEP groove in the rotor and wear a 45* on the coner or the disk. I don't know whats wrong yet but I defentially need new disks and I am planning a trp to the Junkie soon and want to know what to look for.<P>Thanks <BR><P>------------------<BR>Brian<BR> -88 Formula||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phil||09-27-2000||07:05 AM||||Don't know about the rotors but sounds like the problem could be your caliper slider pins, Fiero Store has replacements.||||reg||1
Z||000002||3400Fiero||09-27-2000||12:49 PM||||I'm pretty sure the 88 Fiero rotors are unique to that car.  I am having to replace 2 of mine (front and rear are identical) and am going to replace them with KVR slotted rotors (in the front).  <P>(In addition, I am going to put on KVR performance pads (only marginally more expensive than oem replacement) and braided brake lines.)<P>------------------<BR>1988 Fiero 3400DOHC<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phil||09-27-2000||02:18 PM||||Be careful of performance/racing pads they only work when they're hot. Normal driving usually doesn't get them hot enough to stop well.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Black88GT||09-27-2000||07:30 PM||||Great news.  I just found out that the '93 Cavalier's rotors will indeed fit the rear of the 88 Fiero.  NOT the front.  They sell these rotors at autozone for 15 bucks...<P><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||1
